  "reasoning": "The patient presents with intense shivering (chills), a really high fever, and rashes/red patches on the arms and neck. Analyzing the reference cases, dengue is consistently characterized by this precise combination of high fever, chills/shivering, and skin manifestations such as rashes or red spots on the arms and body. Malaria references emphasize vomiting, sweating, and severe itching but lack any mention of rashes. Chicken pox references highlight itchy bumps and fatigue, typically with mild fever rather than high fever accompanied by severe chills. Drug reaction references focus on flaky peeling skin, hand tremors, and cognitive symptoms like brain fog. Allergy references center on respiratory congestion, sneezing, and facial swelling. Given the specific triad of high fever, pronounced chills, and localized rashes, dengue is the only diagnosis factually supported by the reference vocabulary and patterns.",
  "final_answer": "[DIAGNOSIS]dengue[/DIAGNOSIS]"
